--- Finding (monads_maybe_monad): a list literal holding an int and an error value
--- that is only ever an error (`error[…]` itself, or the answer of a function that
--- only fails) is an internal error in both modes, before the program runs.
---
--- crow run -i and crow run alike (exit 1, nothing printed):
---   monads_maybe_monad-1.co: internal error: the lowered program disagrees with
---   itself — a bug in Crow, not in this program: monads_maybe_monad-1 main: line
---   27: a list of Result(Int) holds an item of type Result(Unit)
--- Expected: 2, nothing, 2 — a list of values that can fail, its second item
--- failed (a list literal may hold values that can fail; an error value is a
--- failed value of whatever kind holds it) — or a refusal in the program's words.
--- The same internal error with a function that only fails:
---   fn nothing[]
---       return error['nothing', 'maybe']
---   fn some[x]
---       return x
---   xs = [some[1], nothing[]]
--- and with the bare int, `xs = [1, nothing[]]`, a refusal that misleads instead:
---   cannot reassign 'xs' from array to array[result[int]] — variable type cannot change
--- on the line that assigns xs for the first and only time. Akin to
--- findings/json_pointer-1.co (an only-error value handed to encode or str does
--- not build compiled), at another site.
--- (Found writing the Maybe monad with Crow's error values as Nothing: a list of
--- Maybe values, one of them Nothing; the solution makes Nothing with a function
--- that can also answer a value.)

xs = [1, error['nothing', 'maybe']]
log[len[xs]]
log[xs[1].reason]
log[xs[0] + 1]
